%% ── Abstract ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
\chapter*{Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}

This thesis investigates how reliability, system stability, and execution performance change when a workflow orchestration system is migrated from a single virtual machine (\vm{}) using a shared process executor to a Google Kubernetes Engine (\gke{}) cluster using the Kubernetes Run Launcher with isolated pod execution. \dagster{} is used as a representative modern workflow orchestration framework. The findings are intended to be directionally relevant for the broader class of pipeline orchestration systems that execute concurrent workloads.

The central research problem is that engineering teams routinely make \vm{}-to-Kubernetes migration decisions based on industry assumptions rather than measured behavioral evidence. The primary contribution of this thesis is the identification and empirical measurement of a \textit{crossover point} --- formally defined as the concurrent workload level at which the \vm{} deployment's job success rate drops below 95\% and the \gke{} deployment's total execution time including scheduling overhead becomes lower than the \vm{}'s execution time under active resource contention.

Five research questions are investigated through six controlled experiments measuring job success rate, execution time variance, failure blast radius, autoscaling response time, Kubernetes scheduling overhead, and infrastructure reproducibility across four defined workload levels. Experiments are repeated three times per level and results reported with standard deviation to ensure statistical credibility.

The study is grounded in four literature pillars: resource contention and performance collapse theory, Kubernetes overhead research, autoscaling response behavior, and distributed systems fault isolation. Infrastructure is provisioned entirely through \cdktf{} in Python and managed via Helm to ensure experimental reproducibility.

\textbf{Keywords:} Kubernetes, GKE, Dagster, workflow orchestration, resource contention, pod isolation, autoscaling, crossover point, CDKTF, reliability measurement, DevOps

