%% ============================================================
\chapter{Introduction}
%% ============================================================

\section{Background and Motivation}

Modern data engineering relies on pipeline orchestration frameworks to automate, schedule, and monitor workflows across variable and unpredictable workloads. A common initial deployment strategy is to host such a framework on a single virtual machine (\vm{}) using a shared process executor. While simple to configure, this architecture contains a fundamental flaw: all pipeline jobs execute within the same process on the same host, sharing a single pool of \textsc{cpu} and memory.

As concurrent job execution increases, resource contention arises. Jobs compete for the same resources, performance degrades non-linearly, and under sufficient load the system fails entirely. Critically, this degradation does not scale gradually --- it accelerates as contention approaches capacity limits, producing a collapse effect rather than a smooth performance curve \parencite[see][]{queueing_theory}.

\dagster{} is used in this thesis as a representative modern workflow orchestration system. It provides a Kubernetes Run Launcher that dispatches each pipeline run as an isolated pod on a \gke{} cluster, where each pod receives defined \textsc{cpu} and memory resources. Failures are theoretically contained within individual pods rather than propagating across the system, and the cluster can expand dynamically through the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (\hpa{}) and \gke{} cluster autoscaler when demand increases.

While this architectural migration is well-documented at the tool level, there is limited empirical evaluation of its actual effectiveness under controlled workload conditions.

\section{The Research Problem}

\subsection{The Surface Problem}

A \vm{}-hosted workflow orchestration system becomes unreliable under concurrent workload because all jobs share the same compute resources. This is an observable and known failure mode.

\subsection{The Deeper Problem --- The Actual Research Gap}

The surface problem is not the research gap. The research gap exists at the intersection of two bodies of literature that have not yet been connected.

Existing research evaluates Kubernetes performance and autoscaling behavior at the \textit{infrastructure level} --- measuring container overhead, pod scheduling latency, and cluster autoscaler response under generic workloads \parencite[e.g.,][]{k8s_overhead_study}. Separately, research on workflow orchestration systems focuses on scheduling algorithms, DAG optimization, and system architecture \parencite[e.g.,][]{airflow_study}. What is missing is empirical measurement of how infrastructure-level Kubernetes mechanisms --- pod isolation, resource limits, and horizontal autoscaling --- actually affect \textit{application-level} workflow execution behavior under controlled concurrent workload conditions.

As \textcite{k8s_overhead_study} demonstrate, Kubernetes introduces measurable overhead at the infrastructure level. As resource contention theory predicts, shared execution environments collapse non-linearly under load \parencite{queueing_theory}. But the specific workload threshold at which migrating from \vm{}-based to Kubernetes-based workflow execution changes the balance between those two forces has not been empirically identified.

This thesis bridges that gap by measuring system behavior at the pipeline execution level rather than at the infrastructure level alone.

\subsection{The Literature Gap Statement}

Existing studies focus on Kubernetes performance and autoscaling at the infrastructure level, but there is limited empirical evidence on how these mechanisms affect application-level workflow orchestration systems under controlled concurrent workload conditions. This thesis addresses that specific gap.

\section{Purpose}

The purpose of this thesis is to design, implement, and evaluate a controlled experimental comparison of single-\vm{} and \gke{}-based \dagster{} pipeline execution, measuring how reliability, system stability, and execution performance change under increasing concurrent workload, and identifying the specific workload threshold at which the \gke{} migration becomes net beneficial.

\section{The Core Idea}

This thesis measures how reliability, failure behavior, execution performance, and system stability change under increasing concurrent workload when a workflow orchestration system moves from a shared execution model on a single \vm{} to an isolated distributed model on Kubernetes. It identifies the specific workload level at which that migration becomes net beneficial.

\textbf{This thesis is not} a proof that Kubernetes is better than a \vm{}. It is not a Kubernetes performance benchmark, a tool comparison, or a cloud provider comparison. It focuses specifically on behavioral differences between two architectural execution models --- shared process execution versus isolated pod execution --- measured at the workflow job execution level under controlled conditions.

\section{Primary Contribution --- The Crossover Point}

The primary contribution of this thesis is the identification and measurement of a \textbf{crossover point}, formally defined as:

\begin{quote}
The concurrent workload level at which \textbf{(1)} the \vm{} deployment's job success rate drops below 95\% due to resource contention, \textbf{AND (2)} the \gke{} deployment's total execution time including pod scheduling overhead and container startup latency becomes lower than the \vm{}'s execution time under active contention.
\end{quote}

This crossover point is not an interpretation --- it is a computable result derived directly from experimental data. It gives engineers a specific, data-backed threshold for when the Kubernetes migration becomes net beneficial.

\section{Research Hypothesis}

Migrating workflow orchestration execution from a single-\vm{} process executor to a Kubernetes Run Launcher on \gke{} will produce measurable reliability improvements through pod isolation and scalability improvements through horizontal autoscaling, but will also introduce measurable scheduling latency and execution overhead that does not exist in the \vm{} deployment.

These advantages are \textit{conditional} and depend on workload characteristics, system configuration, and resource constraints. At low concurrency levels, the \vm{} may outperform \gke{} on execution speed because resource contention has not yet reached critical levels and there is no pod scheduling overhead to pay. At high concurrency levels, \gke{} is expected to outperform the \vm{} as pod isolation prevents the contention collapse that a shared execution model cannot avoid.

If results show the \vm{} performs better across all tested concurrency levels, that is an equally valid and valuable finding.

\section{Research Questions}

\subsection*{Main Research Question}

\begin{quote}
\itshape
How do reliability, system stability, and execution performance change when a workflow orchestration system is migrated from a single-\vm{} process executor to a Kubernetes Run Launcher on \gke{} under increasing concurrent workload, and at what specific workload level does this migration become net beneficial as measured by the formally defined crossover point?
\end{quote}

\subsection*{Supporting Questions}

\begin{description}[leftmargin=2cm, style=nextline]

\item[\sq{1} --- VM Failure Threshold and Degradation Profile]
At what concurrency level does a single-\vm{} workflow deployment fail, and how do job success rate, execution time variance, and resource utilization degrade as concurrent load increases toward that threshold?

\item[\sq{2} --- Pod Isolation Effectiveness and Blast Radius Containment]
To what degree does Kubernetes pod isolation contain failures and reduce execution time variance compared to the single-\vm{} deployment under equivalent concurrent workload levels?

\item[\sq{3} --- Autoscaling Response Speed and Job Impact During Scale-out]
How quickly does the \gke{} cluster provision additional capacity when submitted workload exceeds current node resources, and how many workflow jobs are delayed or fail during the scale-out window before new capacity becomes available?

\item[\sq{4} --- Operational Overhead and the Crossover Point]
What measurable scheduling latency and execution overhead does Kubernetes introduce compared to the \vm{} process executor at each concurrency level, and at what concurrency level does this overhead become smaller than the reliability gain from pod isolation --- the formally defined crossover point?

\item[\sq{5} --- Infrastructure Reproducibility]
Can the complete \gke{} cluster and \dagster{} deployment be rebuilt identically across multiple cycles using \cdktf{} and Helm, and what deviations if any arise between cycles that could affect experimental validity?

\end{description}

\section{How the Questions Form One Argument}

The five supporting questions are not independent. They form a single connected logical chain.

\sq{1} establishes the exact failure profile of the \vm{} architecture --- the baseline evidence. \sq{2} tests whether \gke{} actually fixes what \sq{1} measured as broken. \sq{3} measures the most operationally critical behavior of \gke{} under stress --- the autoscaling window during which jobs are unprotected. \sq{4} synthesizes \sq{1} through \sq{3} into the crossover point --- the primary contribution. \sq{5} validates the experimental environment, which is a prerequisite for all other findings to be credible.

\section{Practical Value and Target Audience}

These findings give small teams, startups, and engineers a data-based answer to the question: \textit{at what workload scale does migrating a workflow orchestration system from a \vm{} to Kubernetes become worth the added complexity and overhead?}

\section{Delimitations}

This study does not include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Paid \textsc{gcp} services beyond the free tier
  \item Comparison with other cloud platforms such as \textsc{aws} \textsc{eks} or Azure \textsc{aks}
  \item Comparison between orchestration tools --- the focus is on execution model behavior, not tool selection
  \item \textsc{ml} model accuracy evaluation
  \item Multi-process \vm{} configurations --- only the default process executor is used
  \item Real or sensitive data --- synthetic workloads only
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Fallback:} If \gke{} free tier prevents stable cluster operation, a minimal single-node Kubernetes configuration will be used instead. This constraint will be documented and analyzed as a finding in \sq{3} and \sq{4} rather than treated as a study failure.

\section{Thesis Structure}

Chapter 2 reviews the four literature pillars underpinning this study. Chapter 3 defines the method, experimental design, and metrics. Chapter 4 presents the results of all six experiments. Chapter 5 discusses the findings in relation to the research questions and the crossover point. Chapter 6 presents conclusions, practical recommendations, and directions for future research.

%% ============================================================
\chapter{Literature Review}
%% ============================================================

This chapter reviews four interconnected research areas that together provide the theoretical foundation for the experimental design. Each pillar connects directly to one or more supporting questions.

\section{Pillar 1 --- Resource Contention and Performance Collapse}
\textit{(Theoretical basis for \sq{1})}

Resource contention arises when multiple processes compete for finite shared resources --- most commonly \textsc{cpu} cycles and memory --- on a single host. Unlike bandwidth-limited degradation, resource contention causes non-linear throughput collapse as load approaches system capacity \parencite{queueing_theory}.

Classic queueing theory \parencite{little_law} demonstrates that as utilisation approaches 100\%, response time approaches infinity non-linearly. For a shared process executor, this means that adding one additional concurrent job beyond the system's stable operating region does not produce a proportional decrease in throughput --- it produces a disproportionately large degradation, which can cascade into complete system failure.

In the context of workflow orchestration, this manifests as individual jobs failing due to \textsc{cpu} starvation or memory exhaustion, which in a shared execution model can cause the orchestration process itself to crash, taking all other running jobs with it. This is the failure mode that \sq{1} empirically measures and quantifies for the specific workload profiles defined in this study.

\textbf{Research connection:} \sq{1} identifies the empirical concurrency threshold at which non-linear collapse occurs for \dagster{} pipeline workloads on a single \vm{}.

\section{Pillar 2 --- Kubernetes Performance Overhead at the Infrastructure Level}
\textit{(Theoretical basis for \sq{4})}

A substantial body of research has evaluated the performance overhead introduced by containerization and Kubernetes orchestration compared to bare-metal or \vm{} execution \parencite{k8s_overhead_study, container_performance}.

The primary sources of overhead in a Kubernetes execution model relevant to this study are pod scheduling latency --- the time between a job being submitted and a pod being assigned to a node --- and container startup time --- the time between pod assignment and the first application instruction executing. These overheads are well-documented at the infrastructure level but have not been measured at the application-level workflow execution layer for pipeline orchestration systems specifically.

Additionally, Kubernetes introduces control plane communication overhead and network namespace isolation costs that do not exist in a native \vm{} process. For short-duration jobs, these fixed costs represent a proportionally larger fraction of total execution time than for long-running jobs.

\textbf{Research connection:} \sq{4} extends infrastructure-level overhead measurement to the application workflow execution layer and identifies at what concurrency level this overhead is outweighed by the reliability gains from pod isolation --- the crossover point.

\section{Pillar 3 --- Autoscaling Behavior and Response Latency}
\textit{(Theoretical basis for \sq{3})}

Kubernetes \hpa{} scales pod count based on observed \textsc{cpu} or memory metrics, while \gke{} cluster autoscaler provisions new nodes when pod scheduling demand exceeds current node capacity \parencite{k8s_autoscaler}. Both mechanisms introduce reaction latency that is absent from a fixed-capacity \vm{} deployment.

Research on autoscaling behavior in cloud environments consistently shows that autoscaling is not instantaneous \parencite{autoscaling_latency}. The reaction window --- the period between a load spike exceeding current capacity and new capacity becoming available for scheduling --- typically ranges from tens of seconds to several minutes depending on cluster configuration and node pool provisioning time. During this window, newly submitted jobs cannot be scheduled and either queue, delay, or fail depending on system configuration.

For workflow orchestration systems, this window has significant operational consequences: jobs submitted during the autoscaling gap are unprotected, and depending on the orchestration framework's retry behavior, they may fail permanently or re-queue after significant delay. This hidden cost of autoscaling is well-documented at the infrastructure level but has not been measured specifically for workflow pipeline workloads.

\textbf{Research connection:} \sq{3} measures the autoscaling response window and its job-level impact specifically for \dagster{} pipeline workloads under a defined spike scenario.

\section{Pillar 4 --- Fault Isolation and Blast Radius in Distributed Systems}
\textit{(Theoretical basis for \sq{2})}

A foundational principle of distributed systems design is that failures should be contained within the smallest possible boundary \parencite{blast_radius}. The concept of blast radius --- the scope of a system impacted by a single failure event --- is central to reliability architecture in microservices and cloud-native systems \parencite{microservices_reliability}.

In a single-\vm{} shared execution environment, the blast radius of any individual job failure is potentially the entire system. A job that exhausts memory can trigger out-of-memory process termination that affects all other jobs running on the same host. In a Kubernetes pod model, each job runs in an isolated namespace with enforced resource limits, meaning a failing pod is terminated by the \textsc{oom} killer without directly affecting other pods on the same node.

This theoretical blast radius reduction is one of the primary architectural motivations for the Kubernetes migration. Whether this reduction holds in practice at the workflow execution level --- and by how much it reduces execution time variance and improves job success rate for concurrently running pipelines --- is an empirical question that existing literature at the infrastructure level does not answer for application-level workflow systems.

\textbf{Research connection:} \sq{2} empirically tests the blast radius reduction at the workflow execution level, measuring whether pod isolation contains failures and reduces execution time variance under equivalent concurrent workload conditions.

\section{Positioning Statement --- The Gap This Thesis Fills}

Each of the four pillars above has been studied independently. Pillar 1 establishes contention theory. Pillar 2 measures Kubernetes overhead at infrastructure level. Pillar 3 documents autoscaling response behavior. Pillar 4 provides the theoretical basis for pod isolation benefits.

What is missing is a study that connects these four pillars at the \textit{application-level workflow execution layer} under controlled concurrent workload conditions. This thesis occupies that intersection, measuring how infrastructure-level mechanisms produce application-level behavioral outcomes for workflow orchestration systems under load, and identifying the specific workload threshold at which the architectural migration produces a net benefit.

%% ============================================================
\chapter{Method and Experimental Design}
%% ============================================================

\section{Research Design}

This study follows a \textbf{controlled experimental design} with a before-and-after comparison structure. The baseline condition is \dagster{} deployed on a single \vm{} using the default process executor. The comparison condition is \dagster{} deployed on \gke{} using the Kubernetes Run Launcher. Identical workloads are applied to both conditions and results are compared across all defined metrics.

The goal is not to create perfectly controlled identical environments, but to measure the combined behavioral effect of the architectural difference under realistic deployment conditions. This reflects how \vm{}-to-Kubernetes migration decisions are made in practice.

\section{Operational Definitions}

\begin{description}[leftmargin=3cm, style=nextline]

\item[Reliability]
Three measurable properties: (1) \textit{job success rate} --- the percentage of \dagster{} runs completing without error at each concurrency level; (2) \textit{failure isolation} --- whether one failing job measurably affects other concurrently running jobs; (3) \textit{\mttr{}} --- time in seconds from failure detection to successful job re-execution.

\item[System Stability]
Measured primarily through \textit{execution time variance} --- the standard deviation of job execution time across repeated runs at each concurrency level. Variance reveals whether system behavior is consistent under load, which mean performance metrics cannot capture. A system with high variance is operationally unstable even if its mean execution time is acceptable.

\item[Scalability]
Two measurable properties: (1) \textit{concurrency capacity} --- maximum concurrent jobs the system sustains without success rate dropping below 95\%; (2) \textit{autoscaling response time} --- time in seconds from spike workload submission to new \gke{} node availability.

\item[Operational Overhead]
Three measurable components: (1) \textit{pod scheduling latency} --- time from \dagster{} job submission to pod entering running state; (2) \textit{container startup time} --- time from pod running state to first job instruction executing; (3) \textit{net execution time delta} --- difference in total end-to-end execution time between \vm{} and \gke{} for identical jobs at identical concurrency levels.

\item[Crossover Point]
Formally defined as the concurrent workload level at which \textbf{(1)} the \vm{} job success rate drops below 95\% due to resource contention, \textbf{AND (2)} the \gke{} total execution time including overhead becomes lower than the \vm{} execution time under active contention. This is a computable result derived directly from experimental data, not an interpretation.

\item[Infrastructure Reproducibility]
The degree to which three consecutive \cdktf{} destroy-and-rebuild cycles produce identical cluster state, measured by configuration comparison and post-rebuild workload execution consistency.

\end{description}

\section{Workload Definition}

Workloads are defined precisely to ensure experimental reproducibility and to simulate realistic pipeline execution resource consumption patterns.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Defined Workload Levels}
\label{tab:workloads}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{lXXXX}
\toprule
\textbf{Level} & \textbf{Concurrent Jobs} & \textbf{Per-Job CPU} & \textbf{Per-Job Memory} & \textbf{Job Type} \\
\midrule
Light  & 1  & 0.25 CPU & 256 MB & Simple data loop \\
Medium & 3  & 0.50 CPU & 512 MB & Data processing \\
Heavy  & 5  & 1.00 CPU & 1 GB   & CPU-bound computation \\
Spike  & 10 & 0.50 CPU & 512 MB & Burst exceeding node capacity \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

Both CPU-bound and memory-bound job variants are included because they trigger different failure modes: CPU contention degrades performance gradually while memory exhaustion causes sudden job termination. The spike level is specifically designed to exceed current node capacity and trigger the GKE autoscaler.

Each workload level is run \textbf{three times} per environment. Results are reported as mean and standard deviation across runs to account for natural system variability.

\section{Infrastructure}

\subsection{VM Deployment (Baseline)}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Google Cloud Compute Engine instance (e2-standard or equivalent free-tier)
  \item \dagster{} deployed with default process executor
  \item No resource isolation between jobs
  \item No autoscaling capability
\end{itemize}

\subsection{GKE Deployment (Comparison)}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \gke{} cluster provisioned via \cdktf{} in Python
  \item \dagster{} configured with Kubernetes Run Launcher via Helm
  \item Per-pod resource requests and limits defined per workload level
  \item \hpa{} enabled for pod-level scaling
  \item \gke{} cluster autoscaler enabled for node-level scaling
  \item All infrastructure defined as code for reproducibility
\end{itemize}

\subsection{CDKTF Stack}

The entire \gke{} infrastructure --- node pools, networking, \textsc{iam} roles, and Helm releases --- is defined in Python using \cdktf{}. This ensures the environment can be destroyed and recreated identically, which is a prerequisite for the reproducibility validation in Experiment 6.

GitHub Actions is used for \textsc{ci}/\textsc{cd}-driven deployment. This is not framed as a GitOps implementation but as a standard automated deployment mechanism.

\section{Experimental Design}

\subsection{Experiment 1 --- VM Failure Threshold (answers \sq{1})}

\textbf{Setup:} Deploy \dagster{} on the \vm{} with default process executor.

\textbf{Procedure:} Run light, medium, heavy, and spike workload levels. Each level is repeated three times. Record job success rate, mean execution time, execution time variance, \textsc{cpu} utilisation, memory utilisation, and \mttr{} at each level.

\textbf{Goal:} Identify the exact concurrency point at which success rate drops below 95\% and document the shape of the degradation curve leading to that point.

\subsection{Experiment 2 --- GKE Pod Isolation Under Equivalent Load (answers \sq{2})}

\textbf{Setup:} \gke{} cluster with Kubernetes Run Launcher. Identical resource profiles to Experiment 1.

\textbf{Procedure:} Run identical workload levels as Experiment 1, three repetitions each. Record same metrics.

\textbf{Goal:} Calculate the delta in job success rate, execution time variance, and \mttr{} between \vm{} and \gke{} at each concurrency level to quantify what changes when jobs move from shared to isolated execution.

\subsection{Experiment 3 --- Failure Blast Radius Test (answers \sq{2})}

\textbf{Setup:} Medium workload running on both \vm{} and \gke{} in separate test runs.

\textbf{Procedure:} After three concurrent jobs are running, deliberately crash one job pod mid-execution using \texttt{kubectl delete pod} on \gke{} and \texttt{kill -9} on the \vm{}. Measure the number of other running jobs affected, time until surviving jobs recover, and their success rate.

\textbf{Goal:} Test whether pod isolation's theoretical blast radius reduction holds in practice under real concurrent workload conditions.

\subsection{Experiment 4 --- Autoscaling Response Under Spike (answers \sq{3})}

\textbf{Setup:} \gke{} cluster at initial capacity insufficient for the spike workload level.

\textbf{Procedure:} Submit 10 concurrent jobs simultaneously, exceeding current node capacity. Measure: (1) time from submission to new node becoming available; (2) number of jobs delayed during scale-out; (3) number of jobs that fail rather than wait; (4) time for full cluster recovery to baseline state. Repeated three times to account for \textsc{gcp} provisioning variability.

\textbf{Goal:} Quantify the autoscaling response window and its impact on workflow jobs during the unprotected period.

\subsection{Experiment 5 --- GKE Overhead and Crossover Point (answers \sq{4})}

\textbf{Setup:} Both \vm{} and \gke{} environments running identical single jobs for overhead isolation.

\textbf{Procedure:} At each concurrency level, record pod scheduling latency and container startup time on \gke{}. Calculate net execution time difference between \vm{} and \gke{} per job per level. Plot both overhead and contention cost against concurrency level.

\textbf{Goal:} Identify the crossover point using the formal definition: the level at which the \vm{} success rate drops below 95\% AND \gke{} total execution time becomes lower than \vm{} execution time under contention.

\subsection{Experiment 6 --- Infrastructure Reproducibility (answers \sq{5})}

\textbf{Setup:} \gke{} cluster and full \dagster{} deployment via \cdktf{} and Helm.

\textbf{Procedure:} Destroy and recreate the complete environment three consecutive times using \cdktf{} destroy and apply. After each rebuild, run the light workload scenario and record results. Compare cluster configuration state and behavioral output across all three cycles.

\textbf{Goal:} Validate that the experimental environment produces identical results across rebuild cycles. Any deviations are documented as findings rather than treated as errors.

