\chapter{Conclusions}
%% ============================================================

\section{Summary of Findings}

[To be completed after experimental phase. This section will summarise each finding in one paragraph per supporting question and then state the primary contribution --- the crossover point --- explicitly.]

\section{Answer to the Main Research Question}

[To be completed after experimental phase. This section directly answers the main research question: how does system behavior change and at what workload level is migration net beneficial?]

\section{Practical Recommendations}

Based on the experimental findings, the following recommendations are offered for engineering teams making \vm{}-to-Kubernetes migration decisions for workflow orchestration systems:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item [To be completed based on actual crossover point finding]
  \item Consider autoscaling response window when designing spike-handling strategies
  \item Use \cdktf{} or equivalent \iac{} tooling to ensure environment reproducibility before migration
  \item Measure execution time variance, not just mean performance, when evaluating system stability
\end{enumerate}

\section{Future Research}

The most natural extension of this thesis is the investigation of how these findings hold when workloads are real \textsc{nlp} transformer-based jobs with variable and unpredictable resource demand patterns. This forms the proposed direction for future master's level research.

Specifically, the master's thesis would deploy Dagster on the \gke{} platform established here, run real \textsc{nlp} pipeline jobs including text ingestion, embedding generation, and model inference, and investigate whether the crossover point identified in this thesis shifts under the specific resource variability patterns of transformer-based workloads.

Additional directions include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Comparison of crossover points across different workflow frameworks (Airflow, Prefect)
  \item Investigation of the effect of node pool configuration on autoscaling response time
  \item Extension to multi-tenant \gke{} environments
\end{itemize}

